# EDLD — .gitignore

# ── User config ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Personal config is never committed — only the example template is tracked.
# Your config.toml contains webhook URLs, user IDs, and personal paths.
config.toml

# ── Auth & key material ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These files contain sensitive cryptographic material — never commit them.
*.key
*.pub

# ── Python ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.pyo
*.pyd

# ── Virtual environments ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.venv/
venv/
env/
.env/

# ── Build / distribution ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
*.egg

# ── PyInstaller ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PyInstaller writes a .spec next to whatever it was pointed at, and those
# generated files are throwaway — hence the original blanket rule.
#
# EDLD's spec is not generated. packaging/edld.spec is the hand-written build
# definition the release workflow builds every binary from, and publishing it
# is also how the LGPL relinking condition is met (see docs/LICENSING.md), so
# it has to be tracked. A blanket *.spec swallows it silently: git says
# nothing, `git add .` skips it, and the failure only surfaces in CI as
# "Spec file not found".
#
# So: ignore stray specs anywhere, but keep the packaging ones.
*.spec
!packaging/*.spec

# ── OS noise ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ── Editor / IDE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# ── Custom themes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Track the directory itself but ignore any CSS files placed inside it.
# Put your personal themes in themes/custom/ and they will never be committed.
themes/custom/*.css

# ── Logs and local data ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.log
